  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{6x+11y}{\\sqrt{y}}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{2y\\sqrt{y}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$",
  "worked_solution": "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{6x+11y}{\\sqrt{y}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6x+11y)\\cdot \\sqrt{y}-(6x+11y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{y})}{{(\\sqrt{y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(11\\cdot \\sqrt{y}-(6x+11y)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}})}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{22y-(6x+11y)}{2\\sqrt{y}})}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{11y-6x}{2y\\sqrt{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=11y-6x.\n$$",
